<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ishtaran</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ishtaran was a Mesopotamian god, the patron of the city of Der on the Elamite border. He was regarded as a divine judge and was invoked in border disputes, including a famous one between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a>. His attendant was the snake god Nirah, and snakes appear on Babylonian boundary stones as his symbol.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ishtaran.jpg" alt="Ishtaran" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marie-Lan Nguyen · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kudurru_of_Eanna-shum-iddina_BM_K.3401.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
